A||||13||qwikgta||Rear hatch strut...(gas strut)...who has them. ||||1||
Z||000000||qwikgta||07-05-2001||11:21 PM||||It seems that may of you have added a gas strut to the trunk lid.  Many because of engine swaps others because of aftermarket spoilers.  I figure that this kit is really easy to do, I don't want to pay someone Big $$ to just give me the correct strut when I can get one from PEP BOYS.  So to those of you who have one, what is the correct part # for the strut, and what does it take to attach it to the deck lid and other end.  IF you have photos of the ends that will work too, I can probably make something or get something in the local yard.  Thanks<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||07-06-2001||10:14 AM||||If you're thinking about Rodney's gas strut kit, it doesn't use a cheap replacement gas strut like you'd find at Pep Boys. I'm also not sure whether you'd be able to find the hardware to hook a strut up yourself that would be similar to the kit.<P>What I would do is take a look in the boneyards at GM mid-size sedans that use the gas struts to hold up the front hoods (I believe Grand Prixs/Centurys use them) and see if you could swap the guts onto your Fiero.<P>Another thing I've seen people do is use a Fiero front hood support on their rear deck lids. <P>Or just buy Rodney's kit and save yourself a lot of hassle.||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||07-06-2001||10:29 AM||||Rodney's kit is like $60bux or something.<P>Get it.  It's great.<P>You COULD reporduce it, after maybe a few hours at $150/hr in a quality machine shop....<P>Some things it pays to do yourself, the strut decklid is NOT one of them.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000003||88CoupeV6||07-06-2001||10:32 AM||||I have Rodney's strut. Great quality. I love it. Look at <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>Josh <B>(known by TheFiero64 for first 1239 posts)</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000004||grinthock||07-06-2001||05:26 PM||||My Rear Deck wo'nt stay up now, because of my intake kit <BR> <A HREF="http://grinthock.2y.net/87intake.html" TARGET=_blank>http://grinthock.2y.net/87intake.html</A> <P>So I ordered the Rodney Dickman dual strut kit today, can't wait for it's arrival!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jake_Dragon||07-06-2001||05:29 PM||||I have the old stand-by Broomstick adapter.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://web.tampabay.rr.com/jakedragon" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/logo.gif"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||07-06-2001||05:45 PM||||You can order hood struts from McMaster Carr Supply Co. with various lengths and compression rates, and several types of ends. They are about $10 each. They also sell mounting brackets. Go to <A HREF="http://www.mcmaster.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mcmaster.com/</A>  and type "gas spring" in the product search, you will get 7 pages of gas springs and accesories. I have used these and they work well. I even used the really big ones to hold up the doors on a Lambo replica.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (driver)<BR>'86 SE (4.5 PFI V8 transplant victim)<BR>'84 coupe (parts)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000007||Philphine||07-06-2001||06:49 PM||||i just got a strut at autozone and cobbled up a bracket (really just a piece of angle iron) and replaced one of the trunk lid bolts with a longer one and a jam nut to let the strut pivot free yet keep the trunk bolt tight. i did this after i put my mustang wing on not quite 2 years ago. it's not perfect. it's kind of strong so i hold the trunk when i open it so it dosen't just fly up. but it's got that did it myself factor and i like it enough that i'll probaly do a couple others (if the same lenth and size will work on a gt) that i have lift problems with eventually. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||qwikgta||07-06-2001||11:10 PM||||Thanks for the input.  I understand that Rodney has a buisness to run and all, I was just trying to do this for under $20 or so.  I figured that someone after they got the kit said that if they had known what it looked like before they bought it they could have made their own.  I have never seen one before so that is why I was asking for someone to "spill the beans" on how to do it cheap.  I have some parts off a few diff GM cars that use the struts so I will make due and figure it out.  Thanks agian<P>Rob<BR><P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||qwikgta||07-07-2001||10:44 PM||||Well just to close this thread, I did it today.  Put a gas strut on my trunk, and I did it for free, ya free.  Best of all I'll tell ya how I did it and you can do it too and save $60.  First I measured the area to figure out what size would work.  Then I went to my local junkyard and looked over all the cars until I found a 84 Buick Regal.  THey use struts on the front and best of all both ends have holes not the round cap end like most had.  The owner said I could have the strut, cause theres no money in a used strut.  I went home and removed the forward bolt on the drivers side of the trunk and put the non strut end on it.  THen I put the other end on just below the rear vent.  If you look under your trunk matting material you will find a small bracket.  I think it is for the cruze control.  MOunt the strut end there with a bolt and bammmmm you are done.  It was free and I have opened and closed it several times and it works every time.  NOw I am going to do the front.  MOre later<P>ROb<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||07-08-2001||04:47 PM||||Cool, I'm all in the "diy" factor, but it would cost me well over $60 to run to the local junkyard and back.<P>Cheaper to work and pay rodney for his machined, welded, and tested kit that works flawless and installed in less than 5minuites :)<P>I'm saving my DIY energy for 4-cam engines :) :)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
